Task: Add a native draggable/resizable chat composer to BlueMind AI on iOS.

We are working only on the iOS version of BlueMind AI.

Before making changes, inspect the current chat composer/input implementation carefully. Understand:

* how the textarea/input currently auto-grows;
* what currently limits its maximum height;
* how scrolling starts inside the input;
* how the keyboard affects its layout;
* whether any legacy web/CSS/JavaScript textarea-resize logic is still affecting the iOS implementation.

⸻

IMPORTANT: WEB CODE MUST NOT BE MODIFIED

BlueMind originally existed as a web app before the iOS implementation, so some old web-specific input/composer logic may still be shared with or affecting iOS.

Do NOT modify, delete, refactor, or break the website implementation.

The web version must continue to behave exactly as it currently does.

If old web code is influencing the iOS composer:

* disconnect it only from the iOS execution path;
* leave the website logic untouched;
* implement the new resizing behavior specifically for iOS;
* if a file/component is shared, use a clean platform-specific branch/component rather than deleting the shared functionality.

If you are unsure whether a piece of code is required by the website, do not guess and do not delete it. Explain it first.

⸻

DESIGN IDEA

I do NOT want to copy ChatGPT’s exact full-screen composer behavior.

ChatGPT currently allows the composer to grow automatically to a certain height and then provides a control to expand further.

BlueMind should use a different interaction:

The user should be able to manually drag the top edge of the composer upward or downward to resize it.

Think of it like a small native iOS draggable sheet handle built into the top center of the input/composer.

⸻

STEP 1 — KEEP NORMAL AUTO-GROW

When the user begins typing, preserve the normal convenient behavior:

* 1 line → small composer
* more lines → composer grows automatically
* continue growing naturally with the content

Do NOT require the user to manually resize the composer for normal short messages.

The composer should initially behave like a normal modern chat input.

⸻

STEP 2 — DEFINE A NORMAL AUTO-GROW LIMIT

Allow the composer to auto-grow until approximately the current useful maximum height / roughly around the middle portion of the available conversation screen.

Do not use a fragile fixed pixel number if the current architecture can calculate this responsively.

It needs to work on different iPhone screen sizes.

Once the normal automatic growth limit is reached:

* stop increasing the outer composer automatically;
* allow text to scroll internally as necessary;
* expose the manual resize control described below.

⸻

STEP 3 — ADD A DRAG HANDLE AT THE TOP

Add a small native-looking resize handle centered at the top edge of the composer.

Visually it should contain:

* a small horizontal rounded line / dash;
* a subtle directional indicator/arrow associated with it so the user understands the composer can be moved vertically.

It should look minimal and polished, similar in spirit to a native iOS sheet grabber.

Do not make it visually heavy.

Do not add a large button.

This is primarily a press-and-drag control.

⸻

STEP 4 — DRAG UP TO EXPAND

When the user presses the handle and drags upward:

* increase the composer height continuously with the finger;
* keep the animation smooth;
* do not jump between sizes;
* keep the keyboard visible;
* keep the text editable;
* preserve the current cursor position;
* keep Plus / Microphone / Send controls correctly positioned.

The composer should be able to expand substantially beyond its normal auto-grow limit.

However, it must stop before colliding with:

* the top navigation;
* status bar / safe area;
* other essential UI.

Calculate the maximum height based on available screen space rather than relying on one device-specific number.

⸻

STEP 5 — DRAG DOWN TO COLLAPSE

When the user drags the same handle downward:

* smoothly reduce the composer height;
* never allow it to become smaller than the normal compact composer;
* preserve the text;
* preserve cursor/selection;
* do not dismiss the keyboard;
* do not delete or reset attached media.

The user should be able to freely move between:

compact composer → medium composer → large composer

by dragging, without requiring a separate full-screen editor.

The movement itself should be continuous, not limited to only three hardcoded sizes.

⸻

STEP 6 — INTERNAL TEXT SCROLLING

This is important.

Composer size and text scrolling must cooperate correctly.

When text is longer than the currently available composer area:

* the text area should scroll vertically;
* the composer itself should remain at the user-selected height.

When the user manually makes the composer larger:

* show more of the existing text;
* do not reset scroll position unnecessarily.

When the user makes it smaller:

* switch naturally back to internal scrolling.

Do not create scroll conflicts between:

1. the conversation;
2. the text inside the composer;
3. the resize drag gesture.

Dragging the resize handle must resize.

Dragging inside the text content must scroll/select text normally.

⸻

STEP 7 — GESTURE AREA

The resizing gesture should activate only from the top resize handle area.

Do not make the entire composer draggable.

This prevents conflicts with:

* text selection;
* cursor movement;
* scrolling;
* attachment interaction;
* microphone/send buttons.

Give the handle a slightly larger invisible hit area than the visible dash so it remains easy to grab.

⸻

STEP 8 — HANDLE VISIBILITY

For the smallest normal composer state, keep the interface clean.

The resize handle can become visible when the composer begins growing significantly / reaches the normal auto-grow region where manual resizing becomes useful.

Once the composer is manually expanded, keep the handle visible so the user understands they can drag it downward again.

Avoid adding unnecessary visual clutter to a one-line message composer.

⸻

STEP 9 — NATIVE IOS FEEL

The resizing should feel native to iOS.

Use:

* smooth interactive movement tied directly to the finger;
* subtle native spring behavior only when appropriate;
* no delayed or exaggerated animations;
* no browser-like textarea resizing;
* no visible CSS-style resize corner.

The user should feel like they are resizing a native iOS surface, not a webpage.

⸻

STEP 10 — KEYBOARD BEHAVIOR

Test carefully with the iOS keyboard open.

The composer must remain anchored correctly above the keyboard.

Resizing the composer must not:

* dismiss the keyboard;
* make the keyboard flicker;
* move the composer behind the keyboard;
* create large empty gaps;
* change the safe-area calculation incorrectly.

Also test keyboard language switching.

⸻

STEP 11 — PRESERVE EXISTING COMPOSER FEATURES

Do not break:

* text input;
* send button;
* microphone;
* Plus/attachment button;
* image attachments;
* file attachments;
* selected media preview;
* transcription;
* current chat mode;
* RTL/Arabic text;
* LTR English/Swedish text;
* text selection/copy/paste;
* multiline text;
* keyboard behavior.

The resizing feature is an enhancement around the existing composer, not a rewrite of unrelated chat functionality.

⸻

STEP 12 — RTL / ARABIC

BlueMind supports Arabic.

Make sure resizing works identically with RTL content.

The resize handle itself should remain centered and should not change position based on RTL/LTR direction.

⸻

STEP 13 — SCREEN ROTATION / SIZE CHANGES

If the device’s available viewport changes:

* recalculate the allowed maximum composer height;
* clamp the current height safely if it is now too large;
* do not lose the user’s text.

Also account for different iPhone screen sizes.

⸻

STEP 14 — STATE BEHAVIOR

Manual resizing should not permanently corrupt the normal auto-sizing system.

Suggested behavior:

* Normal typing starts with auto-grow.
* Once the user manually drags the composer, respect the user’s selected height.
* If the composer becomes empty/new chat and returns to the normal compact state, it can return to standard auto-grow behavior.

Avoid weird cases where deleting text leaves a giant empty composer forever unless the user intentionally expanded it and the current UX expects that.

Implement the cleanest predictable behavior.

⸻

STEP 15 — PERFORMANCE

Dragging must stay smooth.

Do not cause:

* excessive React re-renders;
* expensive layout recalculation every frame;
* conversation list re-rendering continuously;
* keyboard animation stuttering.

Use the most appropriate native/iOS-capable approach available in the current React/Capacitor architecture.

⸻

IMPORTANT ARCHITECTURE RULE

Do not force the old website textarea/resizing implementation onto iOS simply because it already exists.

If the web implementation uses CSS such as:

* max-height;
* overflow rules;
* textarea autosize;
* DOM measurements;
* browser resize events;

and these conflict with the desired native iOS interaction, isolate them from iOS and implement an appropriate iOS solution.

Again: do not delete or modify the web implementation itself.

⸻

VISUAL RESULT

Final BlueMind iOS behavior:

1. User opens chat.
2. Composer begins compact.
3. User types.
4. Composer automatically grows naturally.
5. When it becomes large enough, a small elegant grabber/resize handle appears at the top center.
6. User presses the handle.
7. Drag upward → composer grows smoothly.
8. Drag downward → composer shrinks smoothly.
9. Long text scrolls inside the composer when needed.
10. Keyboard and all chat controls remain stable.

No forced full-screen editor is required.

